+# BLOCKED: 002_unique_email.sql
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` cannot be written as specified without
+modifying or deleting customer rows. Both requirements below are impossible
+to satisfy against `fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql`:
+
+1. `users.email` must be `NOT NULL`.
+2. `users.email` must be unique, ignoring case.
+
+## Blocking data
+
+`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql` (owned by Support) contains:
+
+- A `NULL` email:
+  - `id = 17`, `name = 'Goran Okafor'`
+- Three rows that are the same address case-insensitively
+  (`ana.silva@example.com`):
+  - `id = 1`, `'ana.silva@example.com'`, `'Ana Silva'`
+  - `id = 4`, `'Ana.Silva@example.com'`, `'Dev Silva'`
+  - `id = 23`, `'ana.silva@example.com'`, `'Ana Silva'`
+
+Consequently:
+
+- `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON users (lower(email))` fails with
+  `UNIQUE constraint failed: index ...`.
+- `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON users (email COLLATE NOCASE)` fails with
+  `UNIQUE constraint failed: users.email`.
+- SQLite cannot add `NOT NULL` with `ALTER TABLE`; it requires rebuilding the
+  table and copying rows. The copy fails on the `NULL` row, and filling it in
+  would modify a customer row.
+
+There is no way to add a real `NOT NULL`/unique constraint while leaving
+those rows untouched. Adding a trigger, a partial index, or a conditional
+check would only enforce the rule for future writes; the existing data would
+still violate it, so that would be a workaround rather than the requested
+constraint.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+Support must resolve the data out of band:
+
+- confirm/provide the email for `id = 17`;
+- decide which of `id = 1`, `id = 4`, and `id = 23` is the real account and
+  what to do with the others (merge, reassign, or remove).
+
+Once the snapshot has no `NULL` or case-insensitive duplicate emails, 002 can
+safely rebuild `users` with `email TEXT NOT NULL` and a unique index on
+`lower(email)` without changing customer rows.
+
+## Current state
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` intentionally contains no executable SQL so
+that running the migration cannot alter data or leave a partially applied
+constraint. The production-snapshot test passes because nothing is changed;
+the fresh-DB constraint test remains blocked.
